  "glossaryTitle": "Glossary",
  "glossaryIntro": "Basic terms used in the app. The ⓘ icon next to a term shows its description anywhere in the app.",
  "glossaryOpen": "Glossary of terms",
  "glossaryOpenSubtitle": "1RM, RIR, RPE, volume and more",
  "glossaryAll": "All terms",
  "glossaryInfoTooltip": "What is {term}?",
  "glossaryProgressLink": "What do these numbers mean?",
  "settingsHelp": "Help",
  "glossaryGroupBasics": "Basics",
  "glossaryGroupEffort": "Effort",
  "glossaryGroupMetrics": "Results and records",
  "glossaryGroupProgress": "Progress",
  "glossaryRepTitle": "Rep",
  "glossaryRepBody": "One full movement, e.g. lowering and pressing the barbell once.",
  "glossarySetTitle": "Set",
  "glossarySetBody": "Several reps done without rest, with a rest between sets. “80 kg × 8” is a set of 8 reps with 80 kg.",
  "glossaryWarmupTitle": "Warm-up set",
  "glossaryWarmupBody": "A lighter set before the real ones that prepares muscles and joints. RepTrack leaves it out of volume, records and estimated 1RM.",
  "glossaryWorkingTitle": "Working set",
  "glossaryWorkingBody": "A real training set: any logged set that is not a warm-up. Results and records come from working sets.",
  "glossaryMusclesTitle": "Primary and secondary muscles",
  "glossaryMusclesBody": "Primary muscles do most of the work in an exercise and secondary ones assist. E.g. in the bench press the chest is primary, the triceps and shoulders secondary.",
  "glossaryLoadModeTitle": "Load mode",
  "glossaryLoadModeBody": "Where the load comes from: external (barbell, dumbbells, machine), bodyweight, weighted bodyweight (e.g. a dip belt on pull-ups) or assisted (a band or machine takes weight off). Bodyweight-only and assisted exercises have no estimated 1RM or volume.",
  "glossaryRirTitle": "RIR (reps in reserve)",
  "glossaryRirBody": "How many more reps you could have done when the set ended. RIR 0 means the next rep would have failed, RIR 2 that two were left.",
  "glossaryRpeTitle": "RPE (rate of perceived exertion)",
  "glossaryRpeBody": "How hard a set was on a scale up to 10. RPE 10 is maximal effort, RPE 9 leaves one rep in reserve, RPE 8 two. Roughly, RPE = 10 − RIR.",
  "glossaryOneRmTitle": "1RM (one-rep max)",
  "glossaryOneRmBody": "The heaviest weight you can lift once with good form. It is used to compare strength over time.",
  "glossaryE1rmTitle": "Estimated 1RM",
  "glossaryE1rmBody": "A 1RM calculated from a working set, without lifting a maximal weight. RepTrack uses the Epley formula: weight × (1 + reps / 30), e.g. 80 kg × 8 is about 101 kg. It only uses sets of up to 10 reps, because higher reps make the estimate less accurate.",
  "glossaryVolumeTitle": "Volume",
  "glossaryVolumeBody": "The sum of weight × reps over all working sets, e.g. 3 sets of 10 reps with 60 kg is 1800 kg. It shows how much work you did.",
  "glossaryRecordTitle": "Record",
  "glossaryRecordBody": "The best result in an exercise's history: the highest estimated 1RM, the most reps or the heaviest set for a given number of reps. “≥ 8 reps: 85 kg” means 85 kg is the heaviest weight lifted at least 8 times in one set.",
  "glossaryBestSetTitle": "Best set",
  "glossaryBestSetBody": "The heaviest set in the selected period, and with equal weight the one with more reps. For exercises without weight it is the set with the most reps or the longest one.",
  "glossarySetsPerMuscleTitle": "Sets per muscle group",
  "glossarySetsPerMuscleBody": "The average number of working sets per week that hit a muscle group. A set counts 1 for the exercise's primary muscles and 0.5 for secondary ones. It shows whether a muscle group is being skipped. Muscle-building plans often aim for about 10–20 sets per muscle group per week; treat this as a guide, not a rule.",
  "glossaryPeriodChangeTitle": "Change in period",
  "glossaryPeriodChangeBody": "The percentage difference between the first and the last day with a result in the selected period. In the summary, the arrows compare the period with the previous period of the same length.",
  "glossaryOverloadTitle": "Progressive overload",
  "glossaryOverloadBody": "Gradually making training harder: more weight, more reps or more sets. It is the main way to keep making progress. The charts in the Progress tab show whether your results are going up.",
